.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_BUSOP_BUS_CONFIG 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_busop_bus_config ,
.Nm ndi_busop_bus_unconfig
.Nd provide default nexus configuration operations
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_busop_bus_config
.Fa "dev_info_t *pdip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fa "ddi_bus_config_op_t op"
.Fa "void *arg"
.Fa "dev_info_t **child"
.Fa "clock_t reset_delay"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_busop_bus_unconfig
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fa "ddi_bus_config_op_t op"
.Fa "void *arg"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va pdip , dip
Attached parent nexus.
.It Va flags
Configuration or unconfiguration flags.
.It Va op
Bus configuration or unconfiguration operation.
.It Va arg
Operation-specific child name or encoded driver major number.
.It Va child
Output for a held child node for \fBBUS_CONFIG_ONE\fP.
.It Va reset_delay
Delay or timeout in clock ticks, not microseconds or milliseconds.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions implement default behavior for a nexus driver's \fBbus_config\fP
and \fBbus_unconfig\fP entry points.
They operate on children already described by the framework and applicable
configuration files; bus-specific discovery remains the nexus driver's job.
.Pp
For \fBBUS_CONFIG_ONE\fP, \fBarg\fP is a child name in \fBname@address\fP form and \fBchild\fP
receives a held device-node pointer on success.
Release it with \fBndi_rele_devi()\fP.
\fBreset_delay\fP is a timeout in clock ticks during which discovery of the named
child may be retried.
For \fBBUS_CONFIG_ALL\fP, \fBarg\fP is unused.
For \fBBUS_CONFIG_DRIVER\fP, encode the driver major number in \fBarg\fP using
\fB(void *)(uintptr_t)major\fP.
For the ALL and DRIVER operations, a positive \fBreset_delay\fP delays the start
of configuration by that many ticks.
Pass zero when no delay is needed.
.Pp
The unconfiguration function supports \fBBUS_UNCONFIG_ONE\fP,
\fBBUS_UNCONFIG_ALL\fP, and \fBBUS_UNCONFIG_DRIVER\fP with corresponding \fBarg\fP
interpretations.
It does not return a child pointer.
The operation flags control attachment, removal, and event handling as
described in \fBndi_devi_config(9F)\fP.
.Sh CONTEXT
These functions may be called from user or kernel context.
They may sleep.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP on success and an NDI failure
status otherwise.
Bulk configuration attempts eligible children and can return success even
when individual children fail to attach.
Unsupported operations return \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ndi_devi_config 9F ,
.Xr ndi_hold_devi 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
